--TEST--
`Core\Test::assertThrows` at the root and `Core\Test::assertDoesNotThrow` are one predicate and its negation over every body, and below the root they are not -- `rule:testing/assertions-are-typed` and `rule:testing/runner-is-strict`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The two members read the same three outcomes off one `call_callable`: the
// body returned, the body threw, or the body ended the request. Each was
// written to answer the *middle* one and to hand the third on unchanged, so
// the property that ties them together is that over any body they disagree --
// `assertThrows(…, Throwable::class)` holds exactly where `assertDoesNotThrow`
// fails and the other way about. A member that grew its own notion of "threw"
// -- one that judged a released return value, or that treated an unmatched
// class as no throw at all -- still prints plausibly on its own line, so the
// question is asked of a whole table and the *agreements* are counted.
//
// `Throwable::class` is what makes the pair complementary. It is
// `nvs_hir::errors::TREE`'s root, so the bound accepts every throw the tree
// can carry and the class argument stops narrowing anything. The last count
// below is that claim's other half: name a class inside the tree and the two
// members are no longer complements, because `assertThrows` is a bound and
// `assertDoesNotThrow` has no bound to be the negation of.
//
// The bodies are named static methods for the reason
// `test-assert-throws-is-bounded-on-both-sides-of-the-class-it-names.nvst`
// gives -- a method reference `Class::method(...)` does not exist yet, so
// each row's `callable` is an anonymous function calling one.

final class Ask {
    // The verdict as a `bool` rather than as control flow. A refusal is a
    // `Core\Test\Failure` on both members (`rule:testing/assertions-are-typed`), which is the whole
    // reason one helper can ask either of them.
    public static function throws(callable(): mixed $body, string $class): bool {
        try {
            Core\Test::assertThrows($body, $class);
            return true;
        } catch (Core\Test\Failure $refused) {
            return false;
        }
    }

    public static function quiet(callable(): mixed $body): bool {
        try {
            Core\Test::assertDoesNotThrow($body);
            return true;
        } catch (Core\Test\Failure $refused) {
            return false;
        }
    }
}

final class Ran {
    public static function parse(): void {
        throw new ParseError("the input is not JSON");
    }

    public static function logic(): void {
        throw new LogicError("the caller had it backwards");
    }

    public static function arithmetic(): void {
        throw new ArithmeticError("the divisor is zero");
    }

    // The throw crosses an anonymous function's frame before it reaches the one
    // `call_callable` is watching, which is the edge a member reading its own
    // frame rather than the pending throw would part company on.
    public static function nested(): void {
        callable(): mixed $inner = fn (): void => { throw new IOError("the disk is full"); };
        $inner();
    }

    // Work first, throw after: the body is not one that could only ever throw.
    public static function late(): int {
        array<int> $steps = [1, 2, 3];
        int $sum = 0;
        foreach ($steps as string $step => int $n) {
            $sum = $sum + $n;
        }
        throw new TimeoutError("after 6 steps");
    }

    public static function nothing(): void {
    }

    public static function seven(): int {
        return 7;
    }

    public static function absent(): ?int {
        return null;
    }

    // A container answer, which both members release rather than hand on --
    // a `callable`'s value is the caller's and neither of these is the caller.
    public static function container(): array<string> {
        return ["a", "b"];
    }

    // Threw and caught its own throw, so nothing is pending by the time the
    // body returns. This row is the one that separates "the body contains a
    // `throw`" from "the body threw".
    public static function caught(): string {
        try {
            Ran::parse();
        } catch (ParseError $swallowed) {
            return $swallowed->message;
        }
        return "";
    }
}

array<callable(): mixed> $throwing = [
    fn (): void => { Ran::parse(); },
    fn (): void => { Ran::logic(); },
    fn (): void => { Ran::arithmetic(); },
    fn (): void => { Ran::nested(); },
    fn (): int => { return Ran::late(); },
];

array<callable(): mixed> $returning = [
    fn (): void => { Ran::nothing(); },
    fn (): int => { return Ran::seven(); },
    fn (): ?int => { return Ran::absent(); },
    fn (): array<string> => { return Ran::container(); },
    fn (): string => { return Ran::caught(); },
];

// Every row of both tables asked both ways. `$partition` counts the rows where
// the two verdicts differed, which is the invariant; the two side counts say
// *which* way round each table came out, so a pair that disagreed by both
// being wrong cannot reach 10 here.
int $partition = 0;
int $threwHeld = 0;
foreach ($throwing as string $t => callable(): mixed $body) {
    if (Ask::throws($body, Throwable::class)) {
        $threwHeld = $threwHeld + 1;
    }
    if (Ask::throws($body, Throwable::class) != Ask::quiet($body)) {
        $partition = $partition + 1;
    }
}

int $quietHeld = 0;
foreach ($returning as string $r => callable(): mixed $body2) {
    if (Ask::quiet($body2)) {
        $quietHeld = $quietHeld + 1;
    }
    if (Ask::throws($body2, Throwable::class) != Ask::quiet($body2)) {
        $partition = $partition + 1;
    }
}

echo "partition=", $partition, "/10\n";
echo "assertThrows held on the throwing table: ", $threwHeld, "/5\n";
echo "assertDoesNotThrow held on the returning table: ", $quietHeld, "/5\n";

// The same throwing table against a class inside the tree instead of its root.
// Four of the five threw something `LogicError` does not bound, so on those
// rows `assertThrows` refuses *and* so does `assertDoesNotThrow`: neither holds,
// and the pair is not a partition. Only the root makes it one.
int $neither = 0;
foreach ($throwing as string $n => callable(): mixed $body3) {
    if (Ask::throws($body3, LogicError::class) == false && Ask::quiet($body3) == false) {
        $neither = $neither + 1;
    }
}
echo "below the root, neither holds: ", $neither, "/5\n";
--EXPECT--
partition=10/10
assertThrows held on the throwing table: 5/5
assertDoesNotThrow held on the returning table: 5/5
below the root, neither holds: 4/5
